n pair, and I lose more chips.\n\n**Promising moves**:\n\n1. **Check (0)** – Safest, get to showdown without risking chips, possibly win if opponent has nothing.  \n2. **Small bet (100)** – Could get folds from worse hands (like ace-high, small pairs below 7), but may get called by any better hand (any king with better kicker, Q pair, etc.) and I'd lose more.  \n3. **All-in (-2)** – Extreme bluff, risky given opponent could have a king and would call; unlikely to fold out any better hand since pot is small, could be a huge overbet with weak hand, likely a poor choice.\n\nGiven pot is small and we have very little invested, checking seems best — it’s a weak made hand with some showdown value against total bluffs.  \n\n**Reason**: My hand beats only high cards without a pair. A bet might fold out exactly those worse hands, but make better hands call or raise. With no prior aggression, a river bet looks like a bluff or thin value; but my hand is too weak for value betting, and bluffing on this dry board with a pair is unnecessary — better to check and hope opponent has ace-high or missed draw.  \n\n**Final choice**: Check.\n\n---\n\nEvaluation: Weak pair with poor kicker, minimal value, no need to bloat pot.  \nReason: Checking ensures showdown without risking chips, and my hand may win if opponent missed completely.  \nAnswer: 0"}
